WEBVTT

00:03.880 --> 00:05.600
All right, let's start with the easy stuff.

00:05.640 --> 00:08.320
The first tip is select only what you need.

00:08.360 --> 00:13.320
What I usually see in many queries is that the developers just go and select all the columns from one

00:13.320 --> 00:13.800
table.

00:13.800 --> 00:18.360
And I can tell you I cannot think of one scenario where you need all the columns of one table in one

00:18.360 --> 00:19.040
query.

00:19.040 --> 00:25.440
So for sure in the results we will get like unnecessary columns and of course reading unnecessary informations

00:25.480 --> 00:27.320
gonna make your query slower.

00:27.320 --> 00:29.280
So this is usually a bad practice.

00:29.280 --> 00:35.080
Don't use select star, but instead of that go list all the columns that you need for your query.

00:35.080 --> 00:37.280
So make sure that you only select what you need.

00:37.320 --> 00:42.040
Don't go and select all the columns from one table, and with that you don't risk reading unnecessary

00:42.040 --> 00:44.160
informations from the database.

00:44.160 --> 00:47.640
So always make sure that you select exactly what you need for a query.

00:47.680 --> 00:49.360
Don't go with a star okay?

00:49.400 --> 00:53.480
Tip number two avoid unnecessary distincts and order by.

00:53.520 --> 00:58.840
I have noticed that many developers, as they are writing a lot of queries, they end up by default,

00:59.080 --> 01:02.600
adding always distinct and order by for each query.

01:02.640 --> 01:07.880
And as we review the code and discuss it with the developer, we see that we really don't need to remove

01:07.880 --> 01:12.880
any duplicates in the query because there are no duplicates, and it was only a habit to remove the

01:12.880 --> 01:15.680
duplicates using Distincts and the same thing for the order by.

01:15.680 --> 01:21.000
In many situations, there is no need to sort the data at all, and those operations that distinct removing

01:21.000 --> 01:26.390
the duplicate and sorting the data, they are very expensive operations in your execution plan.

01:26.430 --> 01:29.790
So they're going to take a lot of resources and slow down your query.

01:29.790 --> 01:35.390
So this considered as a bad practice if you always go and use distinct, even though it's not needed,

01:35.390 --> 01:39.350
or you are using the order by in order to sort the data when it is not necessary.

01:39.350 --> 01:41.510
So the best practice here is to avoid them.

01:41.550 --> 01:45.270
Don't use distinct or order by only if it is necessary.

01:45.310 --> 01:49.750
Okay, the next one for exploration purposes, limit the rows.

01:49.750 --> 01:54.270
So sometimes, especially if you are working with a new database, you would like to explore the tables

01:54.310 --> 01:57.830
just to have a quick peek in order to see the content of the tables.

01:57.830 --> 02:03.030
And if your database has a lot of big tables with millions of rows and so on, you will be consuming

02:03.070 --> 02:06.310
a lot of resources if you just select the data like this.

02:06.310 --> 02:09.110
So now imagine that the orders has like 100 million.

02:09.150 --> 02:13.470
As you run this query, the database has to fetch all the 100 million for you.

02:13.470 --> 02:14.910
And usually for exploration.

02:14.910 --> 02:17.910
It's enough to see like ten rows and that's going to be enough.

02:18.070 --> 02:20.790
That's why it is considered as a bad practice.

02:20.830 --> 02:24.430
If you are exploring the tables do not have a limit or top.

02:24.430 --> 02:28.990
So a good practice would be to say select top ten and then have the same query.

02:28.990 --> 02:34.710
So if you go over here, you will get only ten rows and the database will not fetch 100 million.

02:34.750 --> 02:36.470
It can fetch only ten rows.

02:36.510 --> 02:42.310
And now if you are exploring a lot of tables, you will not consume a lot of resource from the database.

02:42.310 --> 02:46.790
So if you are exploring, always limit the number of rows that you are retrieving.
